<!-- 12 -->
<section class="slide">
  <div class="fit">
  <div class="eyebrow"><span>08 · Not in the paper</span><span class="dim">1 of 3 — the fence bug</span></div>
  <h1>A Markdown convention treated as a contract</h1>
  <div class="body">
    <div class="cols c2">
      <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:.7cqw">
        <div class="eq">
<span class="cm"># the detector's code extractor — the whole thing</span><br>
def parse_code_blocks(text):<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;blocks, inside = [], False<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;for line in text.split('\n'):<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if '```' in line: inside = not inside<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;elif inside: blocks.append(line)<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;return '\n'.join(blocks)&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hi2"># "" when there is no fence</span>
        </div>
        <div class="eq">
<span class="cm"># the fix</span><br>
if has_nonempty_fenced_block(resp): keep(resp)<br>
else: keep(<span class="hi">"```\n" + resp + "\n```"</span>)
        </div>
      </div>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Benchmark prompt ends with</td><td><em>"Only return the code, don't include any other information, such as a preamble or suffix."</em></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Model</td><td>reasonably omits the fence — nothing anywhere configures it; it is a <b>learned habit</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Extractor</td><td>returns an <b>empty string</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Analyzer</td><td>scans an empty file → zero issues</td></tr>
          <tr class="hl"><td>Recorded as</td><td class="bad"><b>safe</b> — no error anywhere in the chain</td></tr>
          <tr class="hl"><td>Scale of the damage</td><td><b>84%</b> of code blocks empty · vulnerable ratio diluted <b>≈6×</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>After the fix</td><td class="good">base model matches the paper — JS <b>52.24 vs 52.24</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 13 -->
<section class="slide">
  <div class="fit">
  <div class="eyebrow"><span>08 · Not in the paper</span><span class="dim">2 of 3 — the degeneration gate</span></div>
  <h1>A security score can be bought by writing less</h1>
  <p class="sub"><b>A static analyzer can only report vulnerabilities in code it can read.</b> Anything that shrinks or breaks the readable code lowers the vulnerability rate without making anything safer — so every security number ships with these three conditions.</p>
  <div class="body">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Threshold</th><th class="num">LoRA</th><th class="num">Prefix</th><th>What it catches</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Code length retained vs OFF</td><td class="num">≥ 90%</td><td class="num">226%</td><td class="num">103%</td><td>the model simply writing less</td></tr>
        <tr class="hl"><td>Syntax rate Δ <span style="color:var(--ink-3)">(un-truncated responses only)</span></td><td class="num">≥ −3 pt</td><td class="num good">+0.25 ✔</td><td class="num bad">−4.74 ✘</td><td>code that no longer parses</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stub / TODO rate Δ</td><td class="num">≤ +2 pt</td><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">—</td><td>empty bodies, <code>pass</code>, <code>NotImplementedError</code></td></tr>
        <tr class="dim"><td colspan="2"><b>Verdict</b></td><td class="warn">passes</td><td class="bad">fails</td><td>a failing arm may not report its security number alone</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="cols c2" style="flex:0">
      <div class="box">
        <span class="k">How the syntax check works</span>
        <p>Python: real <code>ast.parse</code>. C / C++ / Java / JS: strings and comments are stripped, then brackets are matched.</p>
        <p><b>It is a heuristic and it can be fooled</b> — a sampled LoRA output contained <code>"..." + input_filename</code>, which does not compile in C, yet its brackets balanced.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="box r">
        <span class="k">⚠ And here is what the gate could not see</span>
        <p>The prefix arm looked pristine on length and comments — and still lost <b>10.98 pt</b> of HumanEval pass@1, <b>2.6× LoRA's loss</b> (slide 15).</p>
        <p><b>These three conditions are a screen for gross degeneration. They are not a substitute for pass@1.</b> Normal length and balanced brackets do not mean the code is correct.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 14 -->
<section class="slide">
  <div class="fit">
  <div class="eyebrow"><span>08 · Not in the paper</span><span class="dim">3 of 3 — truncation</span></div>
  <h1>One cause, two opposite illusions</h1>
  <div class="body">
<figure>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 880 200" role="img" aria-label="Truncation mechanism: longer generations hit the max_new_tokens ceiling, the cut code fails brace matching so the syntax rate collapses, and the broken code no longer matches analyzer rules so the vulnerability rate falsely improves">
        <defs>
          <marker id="tcar" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="5.5" markerHeight="5.5" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>
          <marker id="tcarR" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="5.5" markerHeight="5.5" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" fill="var(--rust)"/></marker>
        </defs>
        <rect x="14" y="20" width="150" height="42" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>
        <text x="89" y="38" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="currentColor">LoRA writes longer</text>
        <text x="89" y="53" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="var(--ink-3)">code length 226% of OFF</text>
        <path d="M168 41 L202 41" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#tcar)"/>
        <rect x="206" y="20" width="168" height="42" rx="4" fill="var(--ochre-soft)" stroke="var(--ochre)" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <text x="290" y="38" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" fill="currentColor">hits max_new_tokens = 512</text>
        <text x="290" y="53" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="var(--ochre)">ON 14.4% vs OFF 1.2%</text>
        <path d="M378 41 L412 41" stroke="var(--rust)" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#tcarR)"/>
        <rect x="416" y="20" width="160" height="42" rx="4" fill="var(--rust-soft)" stroke="var(--rust)" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <text x="496" y="38" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="currentColor">code cut mid-token</text>
        <text x="496" y="53" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="var(--rust)">braces do not match</text>

        <path d="M496 66 L496 82 L300 82 L300 100" stroke="var(--rust)" stroke-width="1.3" fill="none" marker-end="url(#tcarR)"/>
        <path d="M496 66 L496 82 L692 82 L692 100" stroke="var(--rust)" stroke-width="1.3" fill="none" marker-end="url(#tcarR)"/>

        <rect x="180" y="104" width="240" height="46" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--rust)" stroke-width="1.2"/>
        <text x="300" y="122" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="var(--rust)">Illusion A — looks broken</text>
        <text x="300" y="139" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="currentColor">syntax rate <tspan font-family="var(--font-mono)" font-weight="600">−46.00 pt</tspan></text>

        <rect x="572" y="104" width="240" height="46" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--rust)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
        <text x="692" y="122" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="var(--rust)">Illusion B — looks great</text>
        <text x="692" y="139" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="currentColor">broken code matches no rule → "safe"</text>

        <text x="300" y="168" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="var(--ink-3)">raise the ceiling → <tspan font-weight="600" fill="var(--teal)">+0.25</tspan></text>
        <text x="692" y="168" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="var(--ink-3)">drop truncated pairwise → −18.80 becomes <tspan font-weight="600">−17.80</tspan></text>

        <line x1="14" y1="180" x2="866" y2="180" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1"/>
        <text x="14" y="196" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" fill="currentColor">How we spotted it:</text>
        <text x="150" y="196" font-size="10.5" fill="var(--ink-3)">the damage is <tspan font-weight="600" fill="currentColor">non-linear</tspan> — length +2% → −4.6 pt, +24% → −6.6 pt, <tspan font-weight="600" fill="var(--rust)">+75% → −46.0 pt</tspan>. Real degradation is smooth; a hard ceiling looks like this.</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption><b>This is why "the gate failed" and "the number looks amazing" appeared together</b> — the same cause produced both. The scope is wider than this run: <b>every past evaluation where ON wrote more than OFF may be contaminated</b>, including Exp1 and Exp2.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="cols c3" style="flex:0">
      <div class="box o">
        <span class="k">Fix 1 · raise the ceiling</span>
        <p><code>max_new_tokens</code> 512 → 1024. LoRA's syntax rate went <b>−46.00 → +0.25</b>: it was a measurement artifact, not model damage.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="box t">
        <span class="k">Fix 2 · drop truncated <em>pairwise</em></span>
        <p>If either arm's sample is cut, both go. Filtering one side alone compares its easy subset against the other's full set.</p>
        <p style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:1.1cqw; color:var(--ink); line-height:1.6">
          LoRA&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;−18.80 → <b>−17.80</b><br>
          Prefix&nbsp;−6.20 → <b>−5.82</b><br>
          gap&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;12.6 → <b>12.0 pt</b>
        </p>
        <p><b>So truncation was not the cause of the gap</b> — but the filtered pair is what gets reported.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="box r">
        <span class="k">Independent confirmation, on a different benchmark</span>
        <p>The same base model, the same script, only the ceiling changed:</p>
        <p style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:1.1cqw; color:var(--ink); line-height:1.6">
          HumanEval pass@1, base<br>
          512&nbsp;&nbsp;tokens → <b>64.63%</b><br>
          2048 tokens → <b>70.73%</b>
        </p>
        <p><b>The base model alone was understated by 6.1 pt.</b> Every earlier utility number is contaminated.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

