            <tr><td class="mono good">Prefix nvt=16 ✔</td><td class="mono warn">↔ LoRA qkv_proj only</td><td class="num">3,145,728</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 16 -->
<section class="slide">
  <div class="fit">
  <div class="eyebrow"><span>09 · Results</span><span class="dim">both axes</span></div>
  <h1>The prefix loses on security <em>and</em> on functionality</h1>
  <div class="body">
    <div class="cols c2">
      <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:.5cqw">
        <h2>Security · vulnerable-code ratio ↓</h2>
        <p class="sub" style="margin:0; font-size:1.12cqw">100 prompts × 5 samples, C and C++, truncated dropped pairwise. SE ≈ 2.2 pt.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th></th><th class="num">OFF</th><th class="num">ON</th><th class="num">Δ</th><th class="num">relative</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="hl"><td>LoRA · 12.58 M</td><td class="num">49.41%</td><td class="num">31.62%</td><td class="num good">−17.80</td><td class="num good">−36.0%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Prefix · 3.15 M</td><td class="num">49.27%</td><td class="num">43.45%</td><td class="num">−5.82</td><td class="num">−11.8%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <h2 style="margin-top:.3cqw">Functionality · HumanEval pass@1 ↑</h2>
        <p class="sub" style="margin:0; font-size:1.12cqw">164 problems, greedy, <code>max_new_tokens=2048</code>, <b>0/164 truncated</b>.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th></th><th class="num">OFF</th><th class="num">ON</th><th class="num">Δ</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>LoRA</td><td class="num">70.73%</td><td class="num">66.46%</td><td class="num">−4.27</td></tr>
            <tr class="hl"><td>Prefix</td><td class="num">70.73%</td><td class="num">59.76%</td><td class="num bad">−10.98</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="call r">
          <span class="k">Both hypotheses falsified</span>
          <b>H1</b> (a prefix captures most of the security gain) — it captures a third. <b>H2</b> (a prefix costs less utility) — it costs <b>2.6×</b> more. <b>It learns less and damages more.</b>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:.5cqw">
        <h2>Ruled out: this is not a bug</h2>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Suspicion</th><th>Check</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Truncation inflating LoRA</td><td>drop truncated pairwise</td><td class="good">cleared — 12.6 → 12.0 pt</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Truncation deflating pass@1</td><td><code>max_new_tokens</code> 2048</td><td class="good">cleared — 0/164 truncated</td></tr>
            <tr class="hl"><td>Prefix ↔ KV-cache interaction bug</td><td>per-problem inspection of the 27 regressions</td><td class="good">cleared — real capability loss</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pipeline or data error</td><td>LoRA shares every line except the peft config</td><td class="good">cleared — LoRA behaves sensibly</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="box">
          <span class="k">What the 27 regressions actually look like</span>
          <p style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:1.06cqw; line-height:1.55; color:var(--ink)">
            HumanEval/102 &nbsp;checks endpoints only → wrong answer<br>
            HumanEval/106 &nbsp;unbalanced comprehension → SyntaxError<br>
            HumanEval/11 &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--rust)">rewrote the docstring's expected output</span><br>
            &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--rust)">from '100' to '000' to fit its own bug</span>
          </p>
          <p>Well-formatted, well-commented, <b>wrong</b>. 27 regressions vs 9 improvements — net −18 problems. Degraded reasoning and instruction-following, not broken generation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box o">
          <span class="k">Candidate mechanism — not a conclusion</span>
          <p>16 virtual tokens enter <b>every layer's</b> KV cache and shift attention at <b>every position</b> — a global, untargeted perturbation, where LoRA's low-rank update can be surgical.</p>
          <p>Consistent with the sweep: the prefix jumps to chosen ≈ −0.77 within 50 steps and saturates, <b>at all five learning rates</b> — it pays a fixed utility cost before it learns anything.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 17 -->
<section class="slide">
  <div class="fit">
  <div class="eyebrow"><span>10 · Next</span><span class="dim">what is left to settle</span></div>
  <h1>One control stands between this and a conclusion</h1>
  <div class="body">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Run</th><th>Why it is required</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="hl"><td class="mono">1</td><td><b>Prefix nvt=64</b> vs the existing LoRA</td><td>identical parameter count — 12,582,912. The prefix currently trains on <b>a quarter</b> of LoRA's budget, so "the prefix is weaker" and "the prefix had less capacity" are not yet separable</td><td>1 run · 3–4 h</td></tr>
        <tr class="hl"><td class="mono">2</td><td><b>LoRA qkv_proj only</b> vs the existing prefix</td><td>identical parameter count — 3,145,728. The same question from the other side</td><td>1 run · 3–4 h</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">3</td><td>MultiPL-E for both arms</td><td>HumanEval is Python only; the security screen was C/C++ only. <b>Remember <code>--max_new_tokens 2048</code></b></td><td>no training</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">4</td><td>Full security evaluation — 693 × 10 × 5 languages</td><td>the cheap screen covers 2 languages at SE 2.2 pt</td><td>no training</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="cols c2" style="flex:0">
      <div class="box r">
        <span class="k">If parameter matching does not close the gap</span>
        <p>Then H1 and H2 are genuinely dead and the paper's subject changes to <b>the capability limits of prefix tuning for security alignment</b> — a negative result, but a publishable and honest one, and the candidate mechanism on the previous slide becomes the thing to test.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="box t">
        <span class="k">What already stands regardless of the outcome</span>
        <p>A reconstructed evaluation subset the paper never released · a fence-extraction bug that diluted every vulnerability rate ~6× · a truncation artifact that understated the base model by 6.1 pt · a scale-free sweep metric · and a same-pipeline LoRA reproduction landing within the paper's own range.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>



</div>


<div id="bar">
  <button id="prev" aria-label="Previous slide">◀ Prev</button>
  <span id="counter">1 / 17</span>
  <button id="next" aria-label="Next slide">Next ▶</button>
  <div class="track"><div class="fill" id="fill"></div></div>
  <span id="hint">← → or Space to navigate · Home / End to jump</span>
</div>

<script>
(function(){
  var slides = Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('.slide'));
  var counter = document.getElementById('counter');
  var fill = document.getElementById('fill');
  var i = 0;

  function clamp(n){ return Math.max(0, Math.min(slides.length - 1, n)); }

  function fit(el){
    var f = el.querySelector('.fit');
    if (!f) return;
    f.style.transform = ''; f.style.width = ''; f.style.height = '';
    if (f.scrollHeight <= f.clientHeight + 1) return;   // fits already — leave it alone
    var k = 1;
    for (var t = 0; t < 8; t++) {
      k *= 0.97;
      f.style.transform = 'scale(' + k + ')';
      f.style.width  = (100 / k) + '%';
      f.style.height = (100 / k) + '%';
      if (f.scrollHeight <= f.clientHeight + 1) break;
    }
  }

  function show(n, push){
    i = clamp(n);
    slides.forEach(function(s, k){ s.classList.toggle('on', k === i); });
    fit(slides[i]);
    counter.textContent = (i + 1) + ' / ' + slides.length;
    fill.style.width = ((i + 1) / slides.length * 100) + '%';
    if (push !== false) {
      try { history.replaceState(null, '', '#' + (i + 1)); } catch (e) {}
    }
  }

  function fromHash(){
    var n = parseInt((location.hash || '').replace('#',''), 10);
    return isNaN(n) ? 0 : n - 1;
  }

  document.getElementById('prev').addEventListener('click', function(){ show(i - 1); });
  document.getElementById('next').addEventListener('click', function(){ show(i + 1); });

  document.addEventListener('keydown', function(e){
    if (e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey || e.altKey) return;
    switch (e.key) {
      case 'ArrowRight': case 'ArrowDown': case 'PageDown': case ' ':
        e.preventDefault(); show(i + 1); break;
      case 'ArrowLeft': case 'ArrowUp': case 'PageUp':
        e.preventDefault(); show(i - 1); break;
      case 'Home': e.preventDefault(); show(0); break;
      case 'End':  e.preventDefault(); show(slides.length - 1); break;
    }
  });

  var rt;
  window.addEventListener('resize', function(){
    clearTimeout(rt);
    rt = setTimeout(function(){ fit(slides[i]); }, 120);
  });

  window.addEventListener('beforeprint', function(){
    slides.forEach(function(s){ s.style.display = 'flex'; });
    slides.forEach(fit);
  });

  window.addEventListener('hashchange', function(){ show(fromHash()); });
  show(fromHash());
})();
</script>
